DSP.mod(11,{"m11-data-eng":{"id":"m11-data-eng","slug":"data-engineering-concepts","m":11,"title":"Data engineering concepts","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium","prereq":[],"tags":["data-engineering","pipelines"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Data engineering concepts a data scientist should discuss fluently: pipelines, storage architectures, modelling, file formats, orchestration and data quality.","keyTerms":["ETL vs ELT","batch vs streaming","data warehouse","data lake","lakehouse","star schema","slowly changing dimensions","Parquet","Airflow","data quality"],"interviewQs":["ETL vs ELT?","Why is Parquet better than CSV for analytics?","What is a star schema?","How do you ensure data quality in a pipeline?"],"mistakes":["Non-idempotent pipelines that duplicate data on rerun","Skipping data quality checks until the dashboard breaks","Treating the data lake as a dumping ground without schema or ownership"],"practice":"Sketch a daily pipeline that loads orders from an API to a warehouse and list where you would put five data quality checks.","related":["m11-pyspark","m2-concepts","m12-lifecycle"],"points":"- ETL vs ELT: transform before loading vs load raw then transform in the warehouse (modern, e.g., dbt).\n- Batch vs streaming (Kafka, Spark Structured Streaming, Flink).\n- Data warehouse (structured, schema-on-write: BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift), data lake (raw, schema-on-read: S3/ADLS), lakehouse (Delta Lake, Iceberg; Databricks).\n- Modeling: star schema (fact + dimension tables), snowflake schema, slowly changing dimensions (SCD 1/2), grain, surrogate keys.\n- File formats: CSV/JSON (row, text) vs Parquet/ORC (columnar, compressed, predicate pushdown) vs Avro (row, schema evolution, streaming).\n- Data quality: null checks, uniqueness, referential integrity, freshness, range checks, schema validation (Great Expectations, dbt tests), lineage, idempotent pipelines.\n- Orchestration: Airflow (DAGs, operators, schedules, retries, backfills), Prefect, Dagster.\n- APIs and scraping: requests, pagination, rate limits, BeautifulSoup/Selenium, respect robots.txt and ToS."},"intuitive":"<p>Data engineering is the plumbing that brings raw data from where it is created (apps, payments, sensors) to where analysts and models can use it, clean and on time. Think of a city water system: pipes (pipelines) carry water from reservoirs (sources) to treatment plants (transformations) to taps (dashboards and models). Good plumbing is reliable (it runs every day), repeatable (running it twice does not double the water), and monitored (alarms go off when something leaks).</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Data scientists who understand pipelines ship models faster and debug data problems sooner.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Move, clean, store and check data reliably, and make every step safe to rerun.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Data engineering moves data from sources to where analysis can use it. ETL extracts, transforms, then loads into a warehouse; ELT loads raw data first and transforms inside the warehouse, which is common with cloud warehouses. A data lake stores raw files cheaply; a warehouse stores structured, modelled tables for analytics; a lakehouse combines both. Batch processing handles data in chunks, say nightly, and streaming handles events as they arrive, for example with Kafka. Orchestrators like Airflow schedule dependent tasks as a DAG. Good pipelines are idempotent, so rerunning gives the same result, and have data-quality checks. The catch: I choose batch unless the business truly needs low latency, as streaming is more complex.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Why a daily load must be idempotent","html":"<p>A pipeline copies yesterday&#39;s orders (1,200 rows) into the warehouse table <code>fact_orders</code> every night at 02:00.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Version 1 appends:</strong> <code>INSERT INTO fact_orders SELECT * FROM source WHERE order_date = yesterday</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The job fails halfway</strong> after writing 700 rows. The scheduler retries it, and the retry inserts all 1,200 again.</li>\n<li><strong>Count the damage:</strong> 700 + 1,200 = 1,900 rows for that day. 700 orders are now double-counted, and the next morning&#39;s revenue dashboard is about 58% too high for that date.</li>\n<li><strong>Version 2 replaces the day&#39;s slice:</strong> delete that date&#39;s rows, then insert, inside one transaction (or overwrite the date partition). A retry gives 1,200 rows however many times it runs.</li>\n<li><strong>Version 3 upserts by key:</strong> <code>MERGE INTO fact_orders USING staging ON order_id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE ... WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ...</code>. Late-arriving updates, such as a cancellation recorded the next day, are also handled.</li>\n<li><strong>Add a check after the load:</strong> the row count for the date equals the source count, and no <code>order_id</code> appears twice. Fail loudly if not.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Pipelines fail and are retried. &quot;Idempotent&quot; means a rerun produces the same result as a single successful run, which is what makes retries and backfills safe.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-etl-vs-elt-and-batch-vs-streaming","text":"ETL vs ELT and batch vs streaming","level":3},{"id":"h-where-the-data-lives","text":"Where the data lives","level":3},{"id":"h-modelling-for-analytics","text":"Modelling for analytics","level":3},{"id":"h-file-formats","text":"File formats","level":3},{"id":"h-orchestration-and-quality","text":"Orchestration and quality","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>ETL vs ELT:</strong> transform before loading vs load raw then transform in the warehouse (modern, e.g., dbt).</li>\n<li><strong>Batch vs streaming</strong> (Kafka, Spark Structured Streaming, Flink).</li>\n<li><strong>Data warehouse</strong> (structured, schema-on-write: BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift), <strong>data lake</strong> (raw, schema-on-read: S3/ADLS), <strong>lakehouse</strong> (Delta Lake, Iceberg; Databricks).</li>\n<li><strong>Modeling:</strong> star schema (fact + dimension tables), snowflake schema, slowly changing dimensions (SCD 1/2), grain, surrogate keys.</li>\n<li><strong>File formats:</strong> CSV/JSON (row, text) vs <strong>Parquet/ORC</strong> (columnar, compressed, predicate pushdown) vs Avro (row, schema evolution, streaming).</li>\n<li><strong>Data quality:</strong> null checks, uniqueness, referential integrity, freshness, range checks, schema validation (Great Expectations, dbt tests), lineage, idempotent pipelines.</li>\n<li><strong>Orchestration:</strong> <strong>Airflow</strong> (DAGs, operators, schedules, retries, backfills), Prefect, Dagster.</li>\n<li><strong>APIs and scraping:</strong> <code>requests</code>, pagination, rate limits, BeautifulSoup/Selenium, respect robots.txt and ToS.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-etl-vs-elt-and-batch-vs-streaming\">ETL vs ELT and batch vs streaming</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ETL:</strong> extract, <strong>transform</strong>, then load the cleaned result. Classic when the warehouse was expensive. <strong>ELT:</strong> extract, <strong>load raw</strong> data into the warehouse, then transform in SQL there (modern cloud warehouses, tools like dbt): keeps raw data, flexible, reproducible.</li>\n<li><strong>Batch:</strong> process data in scheduled chunks (hourly, daily): simple and cheap. <strong>Streaming:</strong> process events as they arrive (Kafka, Spark Structured Streaming, Flink) for real-time needs such as fraud or live dashboards; more complex.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-where-the-data-lives\">Where the data lives</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Store</th><th>What</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Data warehouse</strong></td><td>structured, schema-on-write, optimised for SQL analytics</td><td>BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Data lake</strong></td><td>raw files of any type, schema-on-read, cheap</td><td>S3, ADLS, GCS</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lakehouse</strong></td><td>lake storage with warehouse-like tables, ACID and time travel</td><td>Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, Databricks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-modelling-for-analytics\">Modelling for analytics</h3>\n<p>A <strong>star schema</strong> has a central <strong>fact table</strong> (one row per event or transaction at a declared <strong>grain</strong>: one order line) connected to <strong>dimension tables</strong> (customer, product, date, store). <strong>Surrogate keys</strong> identify dimension rows independent of source IDs. A <strong>snowflake schema</strong> normalises dimensions further. <strong>Slowly changing dimensions:</strong> <strong>SCD Type 1</strong> overwrites the old value (no history); <strong>SCD Type 2</strong> adds a new row with validity dates, preserving history.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>customer_key</th><th>customer_id</th><th>city</th><th>valid_from</th><th>valid_to</th><th>is_current</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>101</td><td>C7</td><td>Pune</td><td>2023-01-01</td><td>2025-02-28</td><td>false</td></tr><tr><td>102</td><td>C7</td><td>Bengaluru</td><td>2025-03-01</td><td>9999-12-31</td><td>true</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>With Type 2, an order from January 2025 still joins to the Pune row, so historical reports stay correct.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-file-formats\">File formats</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Layout</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CSV / JSON</td><td>row-based text</td><td>interchange; slow, untyped, big</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Parquet / ORC</strong></td><td><strong>columnar</strong>, compressed, typed, with statistics</td><td>analytics: read only the columns you need (<strong>column pruning</strong>) and skip blocks using min/max statistics (<strong>predicate pushdown</strong>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Avro</strong></td><td>row-based with an embedded schema</td><td>streaming and schema evolution</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A query that sums one column of a 50-column table reads about 2% of a Parquet file but the whole CSV.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-orchestration-and-quality\">Orchestration and quality</h3>\n<p><strong>Airflow</strong> defines pipelines as DAGs of tasks with schedules, retries, dependencies and backfills (Prefect and Dagster are alternatives).</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> airflow <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> DAG\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> airflow.operators.python <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> PythonOperator\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> datetime <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> datetime\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">with</span> DAG(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;daily_orders&quot;</span>, start_date=datetime(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2025</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>), schedule=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;@daily&quot;</span>, catchup=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> dag:\n    extract = PythonOperator(task_id=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;extract&quot;</span>, python_callable=extract_orders)\n    validate = PythonOperator(task_id=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;validate&quot;</span>, python_callable=check_quality)\n    load = PythonOperator(task_id=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;load&quot;</span>, python_callable=load_to_warehouse)\n    extract &gt;&gt; validate &gt;&gt; load</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Idempotency:</strong> rerunning a day&#39;s job must produce the same result without duplicates (write to a partition with overwrite, or use <code>MERGE</code>/upsert keyed on a business key). <strong>Data quality checks</strong> (Great Expectations, dbt tests): not-null and uniqueness of keys, referential integrity, <strong>freshness</strong> (data arrived on time), range and accepted-value checks, row-count and volume anomalies, schema validation. Track <strong>lineage</strong> (where each number comes from). Put checks <strong>between</strong> stages so bad data stops early.</p>\n<p><strong>APIs and scraping:</strong> <code>requests</code> with pagination, retries and rate limits; BeautifulSoup or Selenium for pages; respect robots.txt and terms of service.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m11-data-eng~1","q":"Your daily load job failed halfway and someone reran it. The next day the revenue dashboard shows double counts. What went wrong and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>The first run wrote part of the day&#39;s data; the rerun appended everything again, duplicating rows. Make loads <strong>idempotent</strong>: load into a staging table, then <code>MERGE</code>/upsert on a unique key, or <strong>overwrite the partition</strong> for the run date in one atomic step. Add a <strong>uniqueness check</strong> on the primary key and a row-count reconciliation against the source, and alert when checks fail.</p>\n","s":"The job was not idempotent: a rerun appended duplicates; overwrite the day&#39;s partition or upsert by key."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-data-eng~ca0_1","q":"ETL versus ELT?","a":"<p>ETL transforms before loading; ELT loads raw data first and transforms in the warehouse, using its compute and keeping raw data for reprocessing.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-data-eng~ca0_2","q":"What does idempotent mean for a pipeline, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>Re-running a job produces the same result without duplicates, so retries and backfills are safe.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-data-eng~ca0_3","q":"Daily sales dashboard: batch or streaming?","a":"<p>Batch: daily freshness is enough, and it is simpler and cheaper.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m11-data-eng~ta1_1","q":"<p>Batch or streaming? (a) A finance team&#39;s daily revenue report; (b) blocking a card payment that looks fraudulent; (c) a model retrained weekly on clickstream data; (d) a live map of delivery partners.</p>\n","a":"<p>(a) Batch: a daily schedule is enough, and simpler. (b) Streaming, or at least real-time scoring: the decision must be made within a second or two, while the payment is happening. (c) Batch: weekly training does not need second-level freshness; collect the stream into storage and process it on a schedule. (d) Streaming: positions change every few seconds. Choose batch unless the business decision genuinely needs freshness.</p>\n"}]},"m11-pyspark":{"id":"m11-pyspark","slug":"pyspark","m":11,"title":"PySpark","min":40,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium","prereq":["m11-data-eng"],"tags":["pyspark","big-data","spark"],"qa":9,"ai":{"scope":"Spark's execution model (lazy evaluation, narrow vs wide transformations, shuffles), DataFrame code with window functions and joins, and performance tuning (broadcast, skew, partitions, caching).","keyTerms":["driver and executors","lazy evaluation","transformations and actions","shuffle","broadcast join","partitions","repartition vs coalesce","skew","caching","Catalyst","adaptive query execution"],"interviewQs":["Why is collect() dangerous?","What is a shuffle and how do you reduce it?","repartition vs coalesce?","How do you fix data skew?"],"mistakes":["Using Python UDFs where built-in functions exist","Calling collect() on large data","Too few or too many partitions"],"practice":"Write a PySpark query with a filter, groupBy, window rank and broadcast join and mark which steps cause a shuffle.","related":["m11-data-eng","m2-ranking","m3-sql-to-pandas"],"points":"Concepts\n\n- Driver and executors, cluster manager, RDD vs DataFrame vs Dataset, lazy evaluation, transformations (narrow: map/filter; wide: groupBy/join, causing shuffle) vs actions (count, collect, show, write).\n- Partitions, repartition vs coalesce, skew, broadcast joins for small tables, caching/persisting, avoiding Python UDFs (use built-in functions or pandas UDFs), predicate pushdown, partitioned Parquet/Delta writes.\n- Spark SQL, window functions, Catalyst optimizer, Adaptive Query Execution.\n\nQ: Why is collect() dangerous?\nA: Pulls all data to the driver; OOM.\n\nQ: Reduce shuffle?\nA: Broadcast small tables, pre-filter, proper partition keys, avoid unnecessary groupByKey.\n\nQ: Data skew fix?\nA: Salting, AQE skew join, broadcast.\n\nQ: repartition vs coalesce?\nA: Repartition does a full shuffle (increase/decrease); coalesce reduces partitions without a full shuffle."},"intuitive":"<p>When data is too big for one computer, Spark splits it into pieces and lets many computers work on their pieces at the same time. A &quot;driver&quot; plans the work and &quot;executors&quot; do it. Spark is lazy: it does not do anything when you describe steps, only when you ask for a result (an action), which lets it plan the cheapest route. The expensive part is moving data between computers to regroup it (a shuffle), like asking everyone to swap papers to sit by the right group. Most Spark tuning is about avoiding or shrinking shuffles.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> PySpark is asked frequently at Indian analytics and big-data firms.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Lazy plans, narrow vs wide steps, avoid shuffles with broadcast and pre-filtering, and never collect big data to the driver.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Spark processes data too big for one machine by splitting it into partitions across a cluster. PySpark is its Python API. DataFrame operations are lazy: transformations like filter, select and groupBy only build a plan, and nothing runs until an action like count, show or write. Narrow transformations work within a partition; wide ones like joins and groupBy need a shuffle, moving data over the network, which is the main cost. To speed up, I filter early, avoid unnecessary shuffles, broadcast the small table in a join, cache reused data, and watch skew, where one key has most rows. The catch: collect brings everything to the driver, so it can crash on big data.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Follow a Spark job and count the data it moves","html":"<p><code>events</code> is 400 GB across 4,000 partitions; <code>users</code> is 50 MB. The job: filter March events, join to users, count events per city.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Reading and filtering (narrow).</strong> Each executor reads its own partitions and drops non-March rows. No data crosses the network. If the data is partitioned by date on disk, Spark skips the other months entirely (partition pruning).</li>\n<li><strong>Laziness.</strong> Until you call an action such as <code>.count()</code> or <code>.write</code>, Spark only builds a plan. Then it optimises the whole plan, for example pushing the filter down to the read.</li>\n<li><strong>The join, done naively (shuffle join).</strong> Both tables are redistributed by <code>user_id</code> so that matching keys meet on the same machine. Most of the large table travels over the network: the March slice of hundreds of gigabytes.</li>\n<li><strong>The join, done well (broadcast join).</strong> <code>F.broadcast(users)</code> copies the 50 MB table to every executor. With 100 executors that is about 5 GB of copies, and the big table does not move at all.</li>\n<li><strong>The group by city (wide).</strong> Rows for the same city must meet, which needs a shuffle, but after a partial count on each machine only a few numbers per city per partition move.</li>\n<li><strong>Write the result</strong> as Parquet, partitioned by month.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Spark&#39;s cost is the network. Filter early, broadcast small tables, and know which steps shuffle (joins, group by, distinct, order by).</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-core-ideas","text":"Core ideas","level":3},{"id":"h-the-course-query-with-shuffle-points-marked","text":"The course query, with shuffle points marked","level":3},{"id":"h-performance-toolbox","text":"Performance toolbox","level":3},{"id":"h-repartition-vs-coalesce","text":"repartition vs coalesce","level":3},{"id":"h-spark-sql-and-windows","text":"Spark SQL and windows","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Concepts</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Driver and executors, cluster manager, <strong>RDD vs DataFrame vs Dataset</strong>, <strong>lazy evaluation</strong>, <strong>transformations</strong> (narrow: map/filter; wide: groupBy/join, causing <strong>shuffle</strong>) vs <strong>actions</strong> (count, collect, show, write).</li>\n<li>Partitions, <code>repartition</code> vs <code>coalesce</code>, skew, <strong>broadcast joins</strong> for small tables, caching/persisting, avoiding Python UDFs (use built-in functions or pandas UDFs), predicate pushdown, partitioned Parquet/Delta writes.</li>\n<li>Spark SQL, window functions, Catalyst optimizer, Adaptive Query Execution.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> pyspark.sql <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SparkSession, functions <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> F, Window\n\nspark = SparkSession.builder.appName(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;demo&quot;</span>).getOrCreate()\ndf = spark.read.parquet(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3://bucket/orders/&quot;</span>)\n\nres = (df.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">filter</span>(F.col(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;</span>) == <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;delivered&quot;</span>)\n         .groupBy(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;city&quot;</span>, F.date_trunc(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;month&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>).alias(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mth&quot;</span>))\n         .agg(F.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>).alias(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rev&quot;</span>), F.countDistinct(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>).alias(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;users&quot;</span>)))\n\nw = Window.partitionBy(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;city&quot;</span>).orderBy(F.desc(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rev&quot;</span>))\ntop = res.withColumn(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rk&quot;</span>, F.dense_rank().over(w)).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">filter</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rk &lt;= 3&quot;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> pyspark.sql.functions <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> broadcast\njoined = df.join(broadcast(small_df), <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;product_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;left&quot;</span>)</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"11.2#1","src":"c","q":"Why is <code>collect()</code> dangerous?","a":"<p>Pulls all data to the driver; OOM.</p>\n","s":"<code>collect()</code> pulls all data to the driver and can run it out of memory."},{"k":"qa","id":"11.2#2","src":"c","q":"Reduce shuffle?","a":"<p>Broadcast small tables, pre-filter, proper partition keys, avoid unnecessary <code>groupByKey</code>.</p>\n","s":"Broadcast small tables, filter early, use proper partition keys, and avoid unnecessary <code>groupByKey</code>."},{"k":"qa","id":"11.2#3","src":"c","q":"Data skew fix?","a":"<p>Salting, AQE skew join, broadcast.</p>\n","s":"Salting, AQE skew-join handling, or broadcasting."},{"k":"qa","id":"11.2#4","src":"c","q":"<code>repartition</code> vs <code>coalesce</code>?","a":"<p>Repartition does a full shuffle (increase/decrease); coalesce reduces partitions without a full shuffle.</p>\n","s":"<code>repartition</code> does a full shuffle (increase or decrease); <code>coalesce</code> only reduces partitions without a full shuffle."},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-core-ideas\">Core ideas</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Driver and executors</strong>, coordinated by a <strong>cluster manager</strong> (YARN, Kubernetes, standalone, Databricks).</li>\n<li><strong>RDD</strong> (low-level), <strong>DataFrame</strong> (structured with a schema, optimised by the <strong>Catalyst</strong> optimiser), Dataset (typed, Scala/Java). Use DataFrames.</li>\n<li><strong>Lazy evaluation:</strong> <strong>transformations</strong> (<code>select</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>withColumn</code>, <code>groupBy</code>, <code>join</code>) build a plan; <strong>actions</strong> (<code>count</code>, <code>collect</code>, <code>show</code>, <code>write</code>) trigger execution.</li>\n<li><strong>Narrow</strong> transformations (<code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>) work within one partition; <strong>wide</strong> ones (<code>groupBy</code>, <code>join</code>, <code>distinct</code>, <code>orderBy</code>) need a <strong>shuffle</strong>: data is redistributed across the network, which is expensive (disk, network, serialisation).</li>\n<li><strong>Partitions:</strong> the unit of parallelism. Too few underuse the cluster; too many create scheduling overhead.</li>\n<li><strong>Adaptive Query Execution (AQE)</strong> re-optimises at runtime (coalesces shuffle partitions, switches join strategies, handles skew).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-course-query-with-shuffle-points-marked\">The course query, with shuffle points marked</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> pyspark.sql <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SparkSession, functions <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> F, Window\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> pyspark.sql.functions <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> broadcast\n\nspark = SparkSession.builder.appName(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;demo&quot;</span>).getOrCreate()\ndf = spark.read.parquet(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3://bucket/orders/&quot;</span>)\n\nres = (df.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">filter</span>(F.col(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;</span>) == <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;delivered&quot;</span>)                              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># narrow; pushed down to Parquet</span>\n         .groupBy(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;city&quot;</span>, F.date_trunc(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;month&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>).alias(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mth&quot;</span>))   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># WIDE: shuffle by (city, month)</span>\n         .agg(F.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>).alias(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rev&quot;</span>), F.countDistinct(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>).alias(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;users&quot;</span>)))\n\nw = Window.partitionBy(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;city&quot;</span>).orderBy(F.desc(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rev&quot;</span>))                          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># WIDE: shuffle to co-locate each city</span>\ntop = res.withColumn(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rk&quot;</span>, F.dense_rank().over(w)).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">filter</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rk &lt;= 3&quot;</span>)\n\njoined = df.join(broadcast(small_df), <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;product_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;left&quot;</span>)                   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># broadcast join: NO shuffle of the big table</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-performance-toolbox\">Performance toolbox</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Remedy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Large shuffle in a join</td><td><strong>broadcast</strong> the small table to every executor; filter and select columns before joining</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Data skew</strong> (one key has most of the rows, so one task runs forever)</td><td>AQE skew-join handling; <strong>salting</strong> (append a random suffix to the hot key and replicate the other side); broadcast; isolate the hot key</td></tr><tr><td>Python UDFs are slow (row-by-row serialisation between JVM and Python)</td><td>use built-in <code>pyspark.sql.functions</code>, or <strong>pandas (vectorised) UDFs</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Recomputing a reused DataFrame</td><td><code>cache()</code> / <code>persist()</code> (and <code>unpersist</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Reading too much</td><td>partitioned Parquet/Delta writes, <strong>predicate pushdown</strong>, column pruning</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Salting sketch:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> pyspark.sql <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> functions <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> F\nsalted_big = big.withColumn(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salt&quot;</span>, (F.rand() * <span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>).cast(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;int&quot;</span>))                          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># spread a hot key over 10 tasks</span>\nsmall_exp = small.crossJoin(spark.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>).withColumnRenamed(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salt&quot;</span>))               <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># replicate the small side 10x</span>\njoined = salted_big.join(small_exp, [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;key&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salt&quot;</span>])</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-repartition-vs-coalesce\">repartition vs coalesce</h3>\n<p><code>repartition(n)</code> does a <strong>full shuffle</strong> and can increase or decrease partitions (evenly balanced; also <code>repartition(&quot;col&quot;)</code> to co-locate by key). <code>coalesce(n)</code> only <strong>decreases</strong> partitions by merging, <strong>without a full shuffle</strong> (cheaper, but possibly unbalanced). Use <code>coalesce</code> before writing fewer output files; <code>repartition</code> to rebalance or increase parallelism.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-spark-sql-and-windows\">Spark SQL and windows</h3>\n<p><code>df.createOrReplaceTempView(&quot;orders&quot;)</code> then <code>spark.sql(&quot;SELECT ... OVER (...)&quot;)</code>: the same SQL skills from Module 2 apply.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m11-pyspark~1","q":"A Spark job has 199 tasks finishing in seconds and one task running for an hour. What is happening and what do you try?","a":"<p>One partition holds a disproportionate share of rows, usually because a join or group-by key is highly skewed (for example a default <code>null</code> or one giant merchant). Look at the Spark UI&#39;s task-time distribution and key counts; then enable <strong>AQE skew join</strong>, <strong>broadcast</strong> the smaller table if it fits, <strong>salt</strong> the hot key, filter or pre-aggregate the skewed key separately, or change the partitioning key.</p>\n","s":"Data skew on a key: enable AQE skew handling, salt the hot key, or broadcast the small side."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-pyspark~ca0_1","q":"What does &#39;lazy evaluation&#39; mean in Spark?","a":"<p>Transformations only build a plan; computation runs when an action is called, letting Spark optimise the whole plan.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-pyspark~ca0_2","q":"Joining a 1 TB table with a 10 MB table. Optimisation?","a":"<p>Broadcast join: send the small table to every executor, avoiding a shuffle of the big one.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-pyspark~ca0_3","q":"One task runs far longer than the others. Likely cause?","a":"<p>Data skew: one key holds most of the data; fix with salting, broadcast, or repartitioning.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m11-pyspark~ta1_1","q":"<p>One task in a join stage takes 40 minutes while the other 199 tasks finish in 2 minutes. 60% of the rows have <code>country = &#39;IN&#39;</code>, and you join on country. What is happening, and name two fixes.</p>\n","a":"<p>Data skew: all the &quot;IN&quot; rows hash to the same partition, so one task does most of the work. Fixes: broadcast the other side if it is small, so no shuffle is needed; &quot;salt&quot; the hot key by adding a random suffix 0 to 9 on the big side and duplicating the small side for each suffix, spreading the hot key over 10 tasks; or turn on adaptive query execution, which splits skewed partitions automatically.</p>\n"}]},"m11-databases-cloud":{"id":"m11-databases-cloud","slug":"databases-and-cloud-platforms","m":11,"title":"Databases and cloud platforms","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium","prereq":[],"tags":["databases","cloud"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Choosing between relational and NoSQL databases, the CAP theorem, vector databases, and mapping the core cloud services across AWS, Azure and GCP.","keyTerms":["relational","NoSQL","MongoDB","Redis","Cassandra","CAP theorem","vector database","S3","BigQuery","Redshift","SageMaker"],"interviewQs":["When would you choose MongoDB or Redis over PostgreSQL?","Explain the CAP theorem.","What is a vector database?","Which cloud platform do you know and what services do you use for data work?"],"mistakes":["Citing CAP as \"pick any two\" without context","Using a NoSQL store without knowing the access pattern","Listing cloud services without describing what you built"],"practice":"Pick one cloud and draw the architecture for a daily batch pipeline and a real-time inference API using its services.","related":["m11-data-eng","m12-key-concepts"],"points":"- Relational: PostgreSQL, MySQL (ACID, joins).\n- NoSQL: MongoDB (document), Redis (in-memory key-value, caching), Cassandra (wide-column, write-heavy), Neo4j (graph), Elasticsearch (search).\n- CAP theorem: in a partition choose consistency or availability.\n- Vector databases for embeddings (FAISS, Pinecone, Qdrant, Weaviate, pgvector).\n\nNeed - AWS - Azure - GCP\nObject storage - S3 - Blob/ADLS - Cloud Storage\nWarehouse - Redshift, Athena - Synapse - BigQuery\nManaged Spark - EMR, Glue - Databricks, Synapse - Dataproc, Dataflow\nML platform - SageMaker - Azure ML - Vertex AI\nServerless - Lambda - Functions - Cloud Functions/Run\nOrchestration - MWAA, Step Functions - Data Factory - Composer\n\nIndian job market note: AWS and Azure are most common; Databricks + Azure is big in services/analytics firms; GCP/BigQuery is common in startups."},"intuitive":"<p>Different databases are different kinds of storage. A relational database is a tidy set of spreadsheets that follow strict rules and can be combined with joins. A document store keeps flexible records (like JSON) that do not need a fixed shape. A key-value store such as Redis is a very fast sticky-note board for things you look up by name. A vector database stores meaning as numbers so you can ask &quot;find things similar to this&quot;. Cloud providers sell all of these as services, and the main skill is knowing which service plays which role.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers check you can place tools in an architecture, not recite product brochures.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Pick the store by access pattern; know one cloud&#39;s equivalents for storage, warehouse, Spark, ML and orchestration.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Pick the database by access pattern. Relational databases like Postgres give tables, joins and ACID transactions, the default for structured data. NoSQL types: key-value for fast lookups, document stores for flexible JSON, wide-column for huge write loads, graph for relationships. Warehouses such as BigQuery and Snowflake are columnar, built for large analytical scans. On the cloud, the pieces map: object storage like S3 for files, a warehouse for analytics, a managed Spark service for big processing, a model serving service for ML. The CAP theorem says a distributed system under a network partition must choose between consistency and availability. The catch: say what the workload is, transactions or analytics, before naming a technology.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Row store against column store: count the bytes read","html":"<p>Table: 100 million orders, 20 columns of 8 bytes each, about 16 GB. Query: <code>SELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders WHERE order_date &gt;= &#39;2025-03-01&#39; AND order_date &lt; &#39;2025-04-01&#39;</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Row store with no useful index</strong> (a typical transactional database): rows are stored whole, so it reads every row, all 20 columns: about <strong>16 GB</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Column store</strong> (a warehouse such as BigQuery or Snowflake): each column is stored separately, so it reads only <code>order_date</code> and <code>amount</code>: 100M × 2 × 8 bytes = <strong>1.6 GB</strong>, and compression usually shrinks that a lot further.</li>\n<li><strong>Column store, partitioned by month:</strong> it reads only March&#39;s files, about one twelfth: roughly <strong>133 MB</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Why not use the warehouse for everything?</strong> Inserting one order means touching 20 separate column files. The row store writes one row in one place and guarantees the transaction (payment and stock change together). Transactions belong in the row store.</li>\n<li><strong>The usual architecture:</strong> the app writes to Postgres; a nightly or streaming pipeline copies data to the warehouse; analysts and dashboards query the warehouse.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Choose the store by how it is read and written: many small writes need a row store; big scans of a few columns need a column store.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-11-3","text":"11.3 Databases","level":2},{"id":"c-11-4","text":"11.4 Cloud (know one platform reasonably well)","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-database-families","text":"Database families","level":3},{"id":"h-the-cloud-side-by-side","text":"The cloud, side by side","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-11-3\"><span class=\"sec-num\">11.3</span> Databases</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Relational:</strong> PostgreSQL, MySQL (ACID, joins).</li>\n<li><strong>NoSQL:</strong> MongoDB (document), Redis (in-memory key-value, caching), Cassandra (wide-column, write-heavy), Neo4j (graph), Elasticsearch (search).</li>\n<li><strong>CAP theorem:</strong> in a partition choose consistency or availability.</li>\n<li><strong>Vector databases</strong> for embeddings (FAISS, Pinecone, Qdrant, Weaviate, pgvector).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-11-4\"><span class=\"sec-num\">11.4</span> Cloud (know one platform reasonably well)</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th><th>GCP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Object storage</td><td>S3</td><td>Blob/ADLS</td><td>Cloud Storage</td></tr><tr><td>Warehouse</td><td>Redshift, Athena</td><td>Synapse</td><td>BigQuery</td></tr><tr><td>Managed Spark</td><td>EMR, Glue</td><td>Databricks, Synapse</td><td>Dataproc, Dataflow</td></tr><tr><td>ML platform</td><td>SageMaker</td><td>Azure ML</td><td>Vertex AI</td></tr><tr><td>Serverless</td><td>Lambda</td><td>Functions</td><td>Cloud Functions/Run</td></tr><tr><td>Orchestration</td><td>MWAA, Step Functions</td><td>Data Factory</td><td>Composer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Indian job market note: AWS and Azure are most common; Databricks + Azure is big in services/analytics firms; GCP/BigQuery is common in startups.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-database-families\">Database families</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Relational</strong></td><td>PostgreSQL, MySQL</td><td>structured data, joins, <strong>ACID</strong> transactions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Document</strong></td><td>MongoDB</td><td>flexible JSON-like records, evolving schemas</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key-value / cache</strong></td><td>Redis</td><td>sub-millisecond lookups, caching, counters, feature serving</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wide-column</strong></td><td>Cassandra</td><td>massive write throughput, time-series-like data, high availability</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Graph</strong></td><td>Neo4j</td><td>relationships (fraud rings, social graphs)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Search</strong></td><td>Elasticsearch</td><td>full-text search and log analytics</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vector</strong></td><td>FAISS (library), Pinecone, Qdrant, Weaviate, <code>pgvector</code></td><td>similarity search over embeddings for RAG and recommendations</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>CAP theorem:</strong> in a distributed system, when a <strong>network partition</strong> happens you must choose between <strong>consistency</strong> (every read sees the latest write) and <strong>availability</strong> (every request gets a response). Partition tolerance is not optional in distributed systems, so the real choice is C or A <em>during a partition</em>. Relational single-node databases sidestep it; Cassandra favours availability (eventual consistency); many systems let you tune per operation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-cloud-side-by-side\">The cloud, side by side</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th><th>GCP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Object storage</td><td>S3</td><td>Blob / ADLS</td><td>Cloud Storage</td></tr><tr><td>Warehouse</td><td>Redshift, Athena</td><td>Synapse</td><td>BigQuery</td></tr><tr><td>Managed Spark</td><td>EMR, Glue</td><td>Databricks, Synapse</td><td>Dataproc, Dataflow</td></tr><tr><td>ML platform</td><td>SageMaker</td><td>Azure ML</td><td>Vertex AI</td></tr><tr><td>Serverless</td><td>Lambda</td><td>Functions</td><td>Cloud Functions / Cloud Run</td></tr><tr><td>Orchestration</td><td>MWAA (Airflow), Step Functions</td><td>Data Factory</td><td>Composer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Indian market note:</strong> AWS and Azure are the most common; <strong>Databricks on Azure</strong> is big in services and analytics firms; GCP and BigQuery are common in startups. Know one platform reasonably well and describe <em>what you built</em> with it (for example &quot;loaded Parquet to S3, queried it with Athena, scheduled with Airflow, served the model on an ECS container&quot;).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m11-databases-cloud~1","q":"You need to serve real-time features (a user&#39;s order count in the last hour) to a fraud model with a 50 ms latency budget. Where do you store them?","a":"<p>A warehouse or relational database cannot reliably answer within 50 ms at high throughput, so keep <strong>precomputed</strong> features in an in-memory <strong>key-value store</strong> (Redis) or a feature store&#39;s online store, updated by a streaming job (Kafka + Flink/Spark Structured Streaming). Keep the same feature definitions for training (offline store) to avoid <strong>train-serve skew</strong>, set TTLs, and monitor freshness and latency.</p>\n","s":"In a low-latency key-value store such as Redis, updated by a streaming job."},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout example\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Worked example</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p><strong>Architecture scenario.</strong> An online store wants (a) to place orders reliably, (b) a daily revenue dashboard, (c) product recommendations. Orders go into a relational database (Postgres) because they need transactions: stock decrement and payment must succeed together. A nightly job copies orders into a columnar warehouse (BigQuery or Snowflake), where analysts run big aggregations without slowing the shop; the dashboard reads from there. Click events stream through a queue into object storage (a data lake) for model training. A recommender trains on that data and its results are cached in a key-value store (Redis) for millisecond lookups on the product page. Each choice follows the access pattern: transactions, scans, files, fast lookups.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-databases-cloud~ca0_1","q":"Why not run the heavy dashboard queries on the production Postgres?","a":"<p>They would slow down live transactions; analytics belongs in a separate columnar warehouse optimised for scans.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-databases-cloud~ca0_2","q":"Row store versus column store?","a":"<p>Row stores keep whole records together (good for transactions); column stores keep each column together (fast aggregations and compression for analytics).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-databases-cloud~ca0_3","q":"What does CAP say in practice?","a":"<p>During a network partition you must sacrifice either consistency or availability; systems choose by use case.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m11-databases-cloud~ta1_1","q":"<p>Pick a store for each: (a) the current contents of each user&#39;s shopping cart, read on every page view; (b) five years of clickstream for model training; (c) monthly revenue by category for a dashboard; (d) placing an order that must decrement stock and record the payment together.</p>\n","a":"<p>(a) A key-value store or cache, such as Redis: very fast lookups by user id. (b) Object storage (a data lake) in Parquet: cheap, scalable, readable by Spark. (c) A columnar warehouse: large aggregations over a few columns. (d) A relational database with ACID transactions, so the order, stock and payment change together or not at all.</p>\n"}]},"m11-dev-tools":{"id":"m11-dev-tools","slug":"developer-tools-git-linux-environments-docker","m":11,"title":"Developer tools: Git, Linux, environments, Docker","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium","prereq":[],"tags":["git","linux","docker","tooling"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The developer tools data scientists are expected to use: Git workflows and conflict resolution, essential Linux commands, reproducible environments and Docker basics.","keyTerms":["git branch merge rebase","reset vs revert","stash","gitignore","Linux commands","pipes","cron","virtual environments","Docker image vs container","Dockerfile"],"interviewQs":["Merge vs rebase?","git reset vs git revert?","How do you resolve a merge conflict?","Docker image vs container?"],"mistakes":["Committing secrets or large data files","Rewriting shared history","Not pinning dependency versions"],"practice":"Create a branch, make conflicting edits, merge, resolve the conflict, and write a proper commit message.","related":["m11-data-eng","m12-key-concepts","m14-template"],"points":"Git/GitHub\n\n- clone, add, commit, push, pull, branch, checkout/switch, merge vs rebase, stash, reset vs revert, resolving conflicts, pull requests, .gitignore, meaningful commit messages.\n- Never commit secrets/data files; use .env and .gitignore.\n\nLinux and shell: ls, cd, grep, find, head, tail, awk, sed, wc, sort, uniq, chmod, ps, top, cron, pipes, SSH.\n\nEnvironments: venv/conda/poetry, requirements.txt, reproducibility (pin versions, set seeds).\n\nDocker: image vs container, Dockerfile, layers, volumes, ports, docker-compose."},"intuitive":"<p>Git is a time machine and a teamwork tool for code: every save point (commit) is remembered, and branches let you try ideas without breaking the main version, merging them back when they work. The Linux command line is the quickest way to poke at files and servers. Virtual environments and Docker make sure &quot;it works on my machine&quot; also means &quot;it works on yours&quot;: a container is a sealed box holding your code and everything it needs.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> These are table stakes for working with engineers, and interviewers check the basics quickly.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Commit small, branch freely, never commit secrets, and package environments for reproducibility.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Four tools come up. Git tracks changes: I make a branch, commit small logical changes, push, and open a pull request; merge combines histories, rebase replays my commits on top of another branch for a linear history; I never rebase shared branches. Linux basics: ls, grep, find, head, tail, chmod, and pipes to chain commands. Environments isolate dependencies: a virtual environment with a pinned requirements file, so the project runs the same on any machine. Docker packages the code and its environment into an image that runs identically anywhere. The catch: reproducibility is the common thread, so I pin versions, set random seeds, and keep secrets out of the repository.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"A branch, a pull request and a merge conflict","html":"<p>You and a teammate both edit <code>features.py</code> on separate branches.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Start a branch from up-to-date main:</strong> <code>git switch main &amp;&amp; git pull</code>, then <code>git switch -c feature/recency</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Commit small, meaningful changes:</strong> <code>git add -p</code> (review each change), then <code>git commit -m &quot;Add recency_days feature&quot;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Push and open a pull request:</strong> <code>git push -u origin feature/recency</code>. A reviewer comments; you push fixes to the same branch.</li>\n<li><strong>Meanwhile your teammate&#39;s branch is merged,</strong> and it changed the same line. GitHub reports a conflict.</li>\n<li><strong>Resolve it locally:</strong> <code>git switch feature/recency &amp;&amp; git merge main</code>. The file now contains:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD\nWINDOW_DAYS = 30\n=======\nWINDOW_DAYS = 28\n&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; main</code></pre></div>\n<p>   Decide with your teammate which value is right (or combine them), delete the markers, run the tests, then <code>git add features.py</code> and <code>git commit</code>.\n6. <strong>Push.</strong> The pull request is now mergeable.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A conflict is Git asking a human to choose between two edits of the same lines. Small, frequent merges keep conflicts small.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-git-essentials","text":"Git essentials","level":3},{"id":"h-linux-and-shell","text":"Linux and shell","level":3},{"id":"h-environments-and-reproducibility","text":"Environments and reproducibility","level":3},{"id":"h-docker","text":"Docker","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Git/GitHub</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>clone</code>, <code>add</code>, <code>commit</code>, <code>push</code>, <code>pull</code>, <code>branch</code>, <code>checkout/switch</code>, <code>merge</code> vs <code>rebase</code>, <code>stash</code>, <code>reset</code> vs <code>revert</code>, resolving conflicts, pull requests, <code>.gitignore</code>, meaningful commit messages.</li>\n<li>Never commit secrets/data files; use <code>.env</code> and <code>.gitignore</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Linux and shell:</strong> <code>ls, cd, grep, find, head, tail, awk, sed, wc, sort, uniq, chmod, ps, top, cron</code>, pipes, SSH.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Environments:</strong> <code>venv</code>/<code>conda</code>/<code>poetry</code>, <code>requirements.txt</code>, reproducibility (pin versions, set seeds).</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Docker:</strong> image vs container, Dockerfile, layers, volumes, ports, <code>docker-compose</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Dockerfile</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> python:<span class=\"hljs-number\">3.11</span>-slim\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> /app</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> requirements.txt .</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> . .</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CMD</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;uvicorn&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app:app&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--host&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0.0.0.0&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--port&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;8000&quot;</span>]</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-git-essentials\">Git essentials</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Shell</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">git <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">clone</span> &lt;url&gt;; git switch -c feature/churn-model          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># new branch (older: git checkout -b)</span>\ngit status; git add src/ README.md; git commit -m <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Add churn feature pipeline&quot;</span>\ngit push -u origin feature/churn-model                       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># publish; then open a pull request</span>\ngit pull --rebase                                            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># update your branch with the main line</span>\ngit stash; git stash pop                                     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># shelve uncommitted work temporarily</span>\ngit <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">log</span> --oneline --graph --decorate -10                     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># see history</span></code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>merge vs rebase</strong></td><td><code>merge</code> joins two histories with a merge commit (keeps the true history); <code>rebase</code> replays your commits on top of the target, giving a linear history. <strong>Never rebase commits others have pulled.</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>reset vs revert</strong></td><td><code>reset</code> moves the branch pointer back (rewrites history; fine locally); <code>revert</code> creates a <strong>new commit that undoes</strong> a previous one (safe for shared branches).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Resolving a conflict</strong></td><td>Git marks <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>=======</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> in the file; edit to the correct final content, remove the markers, <code>git add</code> the file, then <code>git commit</code> (or <code>git rebase --continue</code>).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>.gitignore</strong></td><td>list files Git must not track: <code>.env</code>, data files, <code>__pycache__</code>, <code>.venv</code>, model binaries.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Never commit secrets or data.</strong> Keep API keys in a <code>.env</code> file (ignored) or a secrets manager; if a secret is committed, rotate it (removing it from history is not enough). Use DVC or cloud storage for large datasets and model files. Write commit messages that say <em>what and why</em> (&quot;Fix leakage by fitting scaler inside CV&quot;).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-linux-and-shell\">Linux and shell</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Shell</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls</span> -lh; <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd</span>; <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">pwd</span>; <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">head</span> -n 5 data.csv; <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail</span> -f app.log; <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc</span> -l data.csv\ngrep -i <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;error&quot;</span> app.log | <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort</span> | <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq</span> -c | <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort</span> -nr | <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">head</span>       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># top repeated error lines</span>\nawk -F, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{sum += $3} END {print sum}&#x27;</span> orders.csv                  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># sum the third column</span>\nfind . -name <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*.py&quot;</span> -mtime -7                                      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># files changed in the last 7 days</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">chmod</span> +x run.sh; ps aux | grep python; top                         <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># permissions, processes, resource use</span>\nssh user@server; scp file.csv user@server:/data/\ncrontab -e     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0 2 * * *  /opt/jobs/run.sh   -&gt; every day at 02:00</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Pipes (<code>|</code>) connect commands so the output of one becomes the input of the next: the UNIX way to build small analytics quickly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-environments-and-reproducibility\">Environments and reproducibility</h3>\n<p><code>venv</code> / <code>conda</code> / <code>poetry</code> isolate dependencies; <strong>pin versions</strong> in <code>requirements.txt</code> (<code>pip freeze</code>); set <strong>random seeds</strong>; record data versions. A project that others cannot rerun is a red flag.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-docker\">Docker</h3>\n<p>An <strong>image</strong> is a read-only template (code + libraries + OS layers). A <strong>container</strong> is a running instance of an image. A <strong>Dockerfile</strong> defines the image:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Dockerfile</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> python:<span class=\"hljs-number\">3.11</span>-slim                          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># base image</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> /app</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> requirements.txt .                        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># copy the dependency list first: this layer is cached until it changes</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> . .                                       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># then the code (changes often, so it comes after)</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CMD</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;uvicorn&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app:app&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--host&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0.0.0.0&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--port&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;8000&quot;</span>]</span></code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Shell</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">docker build -t churn-api .\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 churn-api             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># map container port 8000 to the host</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Key words: <strong>layers</strong> (cached build steps), <strong>volumes</strong> (persistent data outside the container), <strong>ports</strong>, <strong><code>docker-compose</code></strong> (run several containers together, for example API + database).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m11-dev-tools~1","q":"You accidentally committed a file containing an API key and pushed it to GitHub. What do you do?","a":"<p>Treat the key as compromised the moment it is pushed: <strong>revoke/rotate it first</strong> (bots scan public repositories within minutes), then remove the secret from the repository and its history (for example with <code>git filter-repo</code>), add the file to <code>.gitignore</code>, move secrets to environment variables or a secrets manager, and consider a pre-commit secret scanner. Deleting the file in a new commit is not enough because history still contains it.</p>\n","s":"Revoke and rotate the key immediately; then remove it from history and add it to .gitignore."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-dev-tools~ca0_1","q":"Merge versus rebase?","a":"<p>Merge keeps both histories with a merge commit; rebase rewrites your commits on top of the target for a linear history. Never rebase shared branches.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-dev-tools~ca0_2","q":"Why use Docker for a model service?","a":"<p>The image bundles code, libraries and system dependencies, so it runs the same in development and production.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-dev-tools~ca0_3","q":"You committed an API key. What now?","a":"<p>Revoke and rotate the key immediately (history keeps it), then remove it from the code and use environment variables or a secrets manager.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m11-dev-tools~ta1_1","q":"<p>This Dockerfile rebuilds slowly every time you change one line of Python. Why, and how do you reorder it?</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Dockerfile</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> python:<span class=\"hljs-number\">3.11</span>-slim\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> . /app</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> pip install -r /app/requirements.txt</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CMD</span><span class=\"language-bash\"> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;python&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/app/serve.py&quot;</span>]</span></code></pre></div>\n","a":"<p>Docker caches each step and reuses a cached step only if nothing before it changed. <code>COPY . /app</code> copies all your code, so any code edit invalidates that step and every step after it, including the slow <code>pip install</code>. Copy the requirements file first, install, then copy the code: <code>COPY requirements.txt /app/</code>, <code>RUN pip install -r /app/requirements.txt</code>, then <code>COPY . /app</code>. Code edits now reuse the cached install.</p>\n"}]},"m11-bi-excel":{"id":"m11-bi-excel","slug":"bi-tools-excel-and-self-check","m":11,"title":"BI tools, Excel and self-check","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium","prereq":[],"tags":["bi","excel","dashboards"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"BI tools and Excel skills for analytics roles: dimensions vs measures, calculated fields, DAX and LOD basics, Excel lookup and aggregation functions, and dashboard design principles; plus the self-check for Module 11.","keyTerms":["Tableau","Power BI","DAX CALCULATE","LOD expressions","measures vs dimensions","XLOOKUP","pivot tables","SUMIFS","dashboard design"],"interviewQs":["Dimension vs measure?","What does CALCULATE do in DAX?","VLOOKUP vs XLOOKUP vs INDEX-MATCH?","What makes a dashboard good?"],"mistakes":["Putting too many charts and KPIs on one page","Inconsistent metric definitions across dashboards","Using VLOOKUP with approximate match by accident"],"practice":"Build a one-page sales dashboard with KPI cards, a trend and a breakdown, and write the decision it supports.","related":["m11-data-eng","m13-metrics","m3-viz"],"points":"- Tableau/Power BI: data connections, dimensions vs measures, calculated fields, LOD expressions (Tableau) / DAX (CALCULATE, FILTER, time intelligence), filters, parameters, drill-downs, dashboard design (KPI cards on top, trends, breakdowns).\n- Excel: VLOOKUP/XLOOKUP/INDEX-MATCH, pivot tables, SUMIFS/COUNTIFS, IF, text and date functions, conditional formatting, data validation, Power Query basics.\n- Dashboard principles: define audience and decisions first; fewer, well-chosen KPIs; consistent definitions; performance.\n\n- Explain a typical ELT pipeline and where data quality checks belong.\n- Write a PySpark window + join query and explain which steps shuffle.\n- Explain branching and merge conflict resolution in Git."},"intuitive":"<p>BI tools (Tableau, Power BI) turn tables into interactive dashboards for people who will never write SQL. The two building blocks are dimensions (the labels you slice by: city, month, product) and measures (the numbers you add up: revenue, orders). A good dashboard is like a car dashboard: a few clear numbers at the top, a trend, a breakdown, and nothing that does not help you make a decision. Excel remains everywhere in analytics firms, so lookups, pivot tables and conditional sums are expected.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Analytics and services companies test these tools heavily, and communication through dashboards is part of the job.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Start from the decision and audience, keep definitions consistent, and show few well-chosen KPIs.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;BI tools such as Tableau and Power BI connect to data and let people explore dashboards. A good dashboard answers specific questions, with the key metric first, simple charts, filters, and clear definitions of every metric. Excel remains common: know VLOOKUP or better XLOOKUP, pivot tables, SUMIFS and conditional formatting. A pivot table is a groupby: rows are groups, values are aggregates. The SQL mapping holds here too, since a lookup is a left join. Interviewers check whether I can turn a business question into a clear view. The catch: a dashboard nobody uses is waste, so I ask who will decide what from it, and agree the metric definitions first.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"A lookup and a pivot, mapped to SQL","html":"<p>Sheet <code>Orders</code> (columns A to C: order_id, user_id, amount) and sheet <code>Users</code> (columns A and B: user_id, city).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Bring the city into Orders:</strong> in D2, <code>=XLOOKUP(B2, Users!A:A, Users!B:B, &quot;not found&quot;)</code>. For each order it finds the user&#39;s row and returns the city. This is a <strong>LEFT JOIN</strong> of orders to users; &quot;not found&quot; plays the role of NULL.</li>\n<li><strong>Older equivalent:</strong> <code>=VLOOKUP(B2, Users!A:B, 2, FALSE)</code>. The FALSE means exact match; forgetting it returns wrong cities silently.</li>\n<li><strong>Insert a pivot table</strong> with City as rows and Sum of amount as values. That is <code>SELECT city, SUM(amount) FROM ... GROUP BY city</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Add Count of order_id</strong> to the values area: <code>COUNT(order_id)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the total</strong> against <code>=SUM(Orders!C:C)</code>. If they differ, some cities are blank or &quot;not found&quot;, which is an unmatched join.</li>\n<li><strong>The usual #N/A causes:</strong> an id stored as text in one sheet and as a number in the other, or a trailing space (<code>=TRIM()</code> fixes it).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Spreadsheet skills are SQL ideas with a different interface: a lookup is a join, a pivot is a GROUP BY.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-11-6","text":"11.6 BI tools and Excel (essential at analytics and services companies)","level":2},{"id":"c-11-7","text":"11.7 Self-check","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-tableau-and-power-bi","text":"Tableau and Power BI","level":3},{"id":"h-excel","text":"Excel","level":3},{"id":"h-dashboard-design-principles","text":"Dashboard design principles","level":3},{"id":"h-self-check-for-module-11","text":"Self-check for Module 11","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-11-6\"><span class=\"sec-num\">11.6</span> BI tools and Excel (essential at analytics and services companies)</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Tableau/Power BI:</strong> data connections, dimensions vs measures, calculated fields, LOD expressions (Tableau) / DAX (<code>CALCULATE</code>, <code>FILTER</code>, time intelligence), filters, parameters, drill-downs, dashboard design (KPI cards on top, trends, breakdowns).</li>\n<li><strong>Excel:</strong> <code>VLOOKUP/XLOOKUP/INDEX-MATCH</code>, pivot tables, <code>SUMIFS/COUNTIFS</code>, <code>IF</code>, text and date functions, conditional formatting, data validation, Power Query basics.</li>\n<li><strong>Dashboard principles:</strong> define audience and decisions first; fewer, well-chosen KPIs; consistent definitions; performance.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-11-7\"><span class=\"sec-num\">11.7</span> Self-check</h2>"},{"k":"cl","id":"m11-bi-excel.c1","kind":"self","items":["Explain a typical ELT pipeline and where data quality checks belong.","Write a PySpark window + join query and explain which steps shuffle.","Explain branching and merge conflict resolution in Git."]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-tableau-and-power-bi\">Tableau and Power BI</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dimensions vs measures:</strong> dimensions categorise (text, dates); measures aggregate (numbers).</li>\n<li><strong>Connections:</strong> live vs extract/import; data model with relationships (star schema works best).</li>\n<li><strong>Calculated fields:</strong> <code>IF [Status] = &quot;Delivered&quot; THEN [Amount] END</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Tableau LOD expressions:</strong> compute at a chosen level of detail independent of the view: <code>{ FIXED [City] : SUM([Amount]) }</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Power BI DAX:</strong> <code>Total Sales = SUM(Orders[Amount])</code>; <code>CALCULATE</code> evaluates a measure under modified filters:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">Delivered Sales = CALCULATE( [Total Sales], Orders[Status] = &quot;Delivered&quot; )\nSales LY        = CALCULATE( [Total Sales], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR( 'Date'[Date] ) )\nYoY %           = DIVIDE( [Total Sales] - [Sales LY], [Sales LY] )</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filters, parameters, drill-downs, tooltips</strong>, and row-level security for permissions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-excel\">Excel</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lookup a value</td><td><code>=XLOOKUP(A2, Products[ID], Products[Price], &quot;not found&quot;)</code> (modern); <code>=INDEX(B:B, MATCH(A2, A:A, 0))</code>; <code>=VLOOKUP(A2, tbl, 3, FALSE)</code> (the <code>FALSE</code> forces exact match; omitting it silently approximates)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional sums and counts</td><td><code>=SUMIFS(Amount, City, &quot;Pune&quot;, Status, &quot;Delivered&quot;)</code>, <code>=COUNTIFS(...)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Logic</td><td><code>=IF(B2&gt;1000, &quot;high&quot;, &quot;low&quot;)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Text and dates</td><td><code>=TEXT(A2, &quot;mmm-yy&quot;)</code>, <code>=LEFT()</code>, <code>=SPLIT</code> via Text to Columns, <code>=EOMONTH()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Analysis</td><td><strong>pivot tables</strong>, conditional formatting, data validation, <strong>Power Query</strong> for repeatable cleaning</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Why XLOOKUP over VLOOKUP:</strong> looks left as well as right, exact match by default, no fragile column index numbers, custom not-found value.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-dashboard-design-principles\">Dashboard design principles</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Define the audience and the decision first.</strong> An executive wants three KPIs and a trend; an analyst wants filters and detail.</li>\n<li><strong>Fewer, well-chosen KPIs</strong> with consistent definitions (the same &quot;active user&quot; everywhere); one metric dictionary.</li>\n<li><strong>Layout:</strong> KPI cards on top, trends next, breakdowns below; most important at top-left.</li>\n<li><strong>Right chart for the question</strong>, consistent colours, clear titles that state the takeaway.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance:</strong> aggregate in the warehouse, limit visuals per page, use extracts.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-self-check-for-module-11\">Self-check for Module 11</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain a typical ELT pipeline and where data quality checks belong.</li>\n<li>Write a PySpark window + join query and explain which steps shuffle.</li>\n<li>Explain branching and merge conflict resolution in Git.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m11-bi-excel~1","q":"A stakeholder asks for &quot;all the numbers&quot; on one dashboard. What do you do?","a":"<p>Interview the stakeholder about the <strong>decisions</strong> the dashboard should enable and how often they will look at it. Propose a one-page summary with a few KPIs, a trend and the top breakdowns, each with a precise definition, plus secondary pages for detail. A dashboard that shows everything supports no decision; document the metric definitions and owners so numbers agree across reports.</p>\n","s":"Ask what decisions it supports, agree on 3 to 5 KPIs, and offer drill-down pages for the rest."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-bi-excel~ca0_1","q":"Excel pivot table equals which SQL operation?","a":"<p>GROUP BY with aggregates, with the column field making it a pivot (conditional aggregation).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-bi-excel~ca0_2","q":"What makes a dashboard useful?","a":"<p>It answers a specific decision, shows the key metric first, uses consistent definitions and simple charts, and has the right filters.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m11-bi-excel~ca0_3","q":"VLOOKUP returns #N/A for some rows. Likely reasons?","a":"<p>The key is missing in the lookup table, or type and whitespace mismatches (text vs number, trailing space); also an exact-match flag omitted.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m11-bi-excel~ta1_1","q":"<p>Write the Excel formula for &quot;total amount of delivered orders in Pune&quot;, with amounts in C, city in D and status in E. What is the SQL equivalent?</p>\n","a":"<p><code>=SUMIFS(C:C, D:D, &quot;Pune&quot;, E:E, &quot;delivered&quot;)</code>. SUMIFS adds column C only where every condition holds. SQL: <code>SELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders_with_city WHERE city = &#39;Pune&#39; AND status = &#39;delivered&#39;</code>.</p>\n"}]}});